-- title:
--   Zero-scheme ideal of a section commutes with base change
-- statement:
--   Let $F \colon X' \to X$ be a morphism of schemes, let $M$ be an $\mathcal{O}_X$-module (an object of `X.Modules`), and assume `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible M`, i.e. every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit module $\mathcal{O}_U$. Let $s \colon \mathbf{1} \to M$ be a section, that is a morphism from the monoidal unit $\mathcal{O}_X$ of `X.Modules` to $M$. For such a section, `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal s` is the ideal sheaf datum on $X$ obtained as the infimum of all $J$ with the property that for every affine open $U$ of $X$ the ideal `coeffIdeal s U` of $\Gamma(X, U)$, generated by the range of the coefficient map `coeff s U`, is contained in $J$'s ideal at $U$; and `Scheme.Modules.pullbackSection F s` is the section $\mathbf{1} \to F^{*}M$ of the pullback of $M$ given by the inverse of the canonical isomorphism $F^{*}\mathcal{O}_X \cong \mathcal{O}_{X'}$ followed by $F^{*}s$. The assertion is that the comap (pullback) along $F$ of the ideal sheaf datum `zeroSchemeIdeal s` coincides with the ideal sheaf datum `zeroSchemeIdeal` of the pulled-back section `pullbackSection F s` on $X'$. No flatness or other hypothesis on $F$ is imposed.
--
--   This is the statement that formation of the zero scheme of a section of a line bundle is compatible with arbitrary base change, so that the closed subscheme $Z(s) \subseteq X$ pulls back to $Z(F^{*}s) \subseteq X'$. It is used throughout the treatment of relative effective Cartier divisors and the relative Picard functor, for instance to recognise that the zero scheme of a section is a divisor on each fibre and to obtain naturality in the base of constructions involving $Z(s)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Modules_IsInvertible_comap_zeroSchemeIdeal.lean

